You can use a loop to run the same code on multiple variables in C++. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    // Using a loop to run the same code on multiple variables
    for (auto& var : {a, b, c}) {
        std::cout << "Value: " << var << std::endl;
        // Run the same code on each variable
        var *= 2;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the loop iterates over a list of variables `a`, `b`, and `c`, and runs the same code on each one. The `auto& var` syntax allows the loop to work with any type of variable.

Alternatively, you can use a range-based for loop with a lambda function to run the same code on multiple variables:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    // Using a range-based for loop with a lambda function
    for (auto& var : {a, b, c}) {
        [](auto& x) { x *= 2; }(var);
        std::cout << "Value: " << var << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This approach is more concise and expressive, but it requires C++14 or later.

